+### [NeRFReN](11981-nerfren.md)
+Separates the radiance field into transmitted and reflected components to handle reflective surfaces like glass and mirrors, modeling a reflection fraction $\beta$ derived from transmitted geometry.
+
+### MobileNeRF
+A framework training a NeRF-like model based on a polygonal mesh with color, feature, and opacity MLPs attached to each mesh vertex [S1]. Alpha values are discretized, and features are super-sampled for anti-aliasing [S1]. During rendering, the mesh with associated features and opacities is rasterized according to the viewing position, and a small MLP is used to shade each pixel [S1]. The method demonstrated a speed approximately 10 times faster than SNeRG [S1].
+
+### Neural Body
+A method applying NeRF volume rendering to human avatars with moving poses from videos, using a vertex-based deformable human body model (SMPL) [S1]. A 16-dimensional latent code Z is attached to each vertex of the SMPL model [S1]. Standard NeRF approaches struggled with moving bodies, whereas the mesh deformation approach of Neural Body was able to interpolate between frames and between poses [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The canonical architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S1]. It was developed to overcome the limitations of explicit 3D representations (like meshes) and implicit scalar fields (like SDFs) by jointly modeling geometry and view-dependent appearance. The use of positional encoding was motivated by the need to help MLPs learn high-frequency details, a technique previously used in Fourier Features for networks (FFNets) [S3].
+The canonical MLP-based radiance field representation was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S1, Parent]. This work established the use of positional encoding and coupled density/color MLPs as the standard architecture. Subsequent developments, such as MobileNeRF (June 2022) and Neural Body (Dec 2020), extended this architecture to mesh-based representations and deformable human avatars, respectively [S1].
 
